.Dd July 18, 2004
.Dt DISKARBITRATIOND 8
.Os Darwin
.Sh NAME
.Nm diskarbitrationd
.Nd disk arbitration daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
listens for connections from clients, notifies clients
of the appearance of disks and filesystems, and governs the mounting of
filesystems and the claiming of disks amongst clients.
.Pp
.Nm
is accessed via the Disk Arbitration framework.
This command is not intended to be invoked directly.
.Pp
Options:
.Bl -tag -width -indent
.It Fl d
Report detailed information in
.Pa /var/log/diskarbitrationd.log .
.El
.Pp
The file
.Pa /etc/fstab
is consulted for user-defined mount points, indexed by filesystem,
in the mount point determination for a filesystem.
Each filesystem can be identified by its UUID or by its label,
using the constructs ``UUID'' or ``LABEL'', respectively.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset ind
UUID=DF000C7E-AE0C-3B15-B730-DFD2EF15CB91 /export ufs     ro
UUID=FAB060E9-79F7-33FF-BE85-E1D3ABD3EDEA none    msdosfs rw,noauto
LABEL=The\\040Volume\\040Name\\040Is\\040This none    msdosfs ro
.Ed
.Pp
On NextBSD the file is a policy overlay on disks the daemon has already
found; it is never a list of things to mount, and lines naming a device
path
.Pq Pa /dev/da0p1 ,
.Li tmpfs ,
an NFS export or swap are ignored.
The first line, in file order, that matches a volume wins.
``UUID'' matches the volume UUID or the GPT partition UUID;
``LABEL'' matches the volume name exactly;
``DEVICE'', followed by an XML property list dictionary, matches when
every key equals the disk's description value.
The mount point
.Li none
means the default
.Pa /Volumes/ Ns Ar name ;
any other mount point must already exist.
The filesystem type
.Li auto
matches any filesystem; anything else must equal the
.Xr mount 8
type
.Pq Li msdosfs , ufs , cd9660 , udf , exfat , ntfs .
.Li auto
and
.Li noauto
turn the automatic mount on or off; every other option is passed to
.Xr mount 8 .
The file is re-read, when it has changed, at the next disk event.
An
.Li auto
line also mounts the volume before anybody logs in.
.Ss Console user
Removable and external media are not mounted automatically until a user
is logged in at the console, as on a Mac at its login window.
The console user is the
.Li State:/Users/ConsoleUser
key of
.Xr configd 8 ,
which a login program publishes with
.Xr consoleuser 8 .
When somebody logs in, the deferred volumes are mounted, owned by the
console user, who also owns the whole removable and external disks and
may mount, unmount and eject them.
When that user logs out, their volumes are unmounted and their disks
ejected; with nobody logged in, mounted volumes that would have been
deferred are unmounted.
Internal media, volumes mounted before
.Nm
reached them (the root filesystem,
.Pa /etc/fstab
at boot) and disks carrying such a volume are left alone.
.Li AutomountDisksWithoutUserLogin
set to true in
.Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/autodiskmount.plist
mounts removable and external media without a login, owned by root.
NextBSD ships the file with it set, together with
.Li DAMountTrustExternal
false so external media keep the untrusted mount options, because no
login program publishes the console user yet.
Once one runs
.Xr consoleuser 8 ,
removing the key restores Apple's defaults (removable and external
media wait for a login); setting it to false defers removable media only
and trusts external media, as on Apple.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/fstab -compact
.It Pa /etc/fstab
.It Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/SystemConfiguration/autodiskmount.plist
.It Pa /var/log/diskarbitrationd.log
.It Pa /var/run/diskarbitrationd.pid
.It Pa /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.apple.diskarbitrationd.plist
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr configd 8 ,
.Xr consoleuser 8
